  },
  "sentences": [
   {
    "role": "hook",
    "text": "If you put a fan behind a cold coil, you have an air conditioner."
   },
   {
    "role": "problem",
    "text": "But the cold coil pulls water from the air, and it drips onto the floor."
   },
   {
    "role": "fix1",
    "text": "So you add a tray and a hose that drains the water away."
   },
   {
    "role": "problem2",
    "text": "But now the coil warms up, and warm air blows into the room."
   },
   {
    "role": "fix2",
    "text": "So you add a compressor that pumps coolant to keep the coil cold."
   },
   {
    "role": "name",
    "text": "This is an air conditioner."
   },
   {
    "role": "how",
    "text": "The heat goes to a hot coil and out the window."
   },
   {
    "role": "myth",
    "text": "And comfort is not why air conditioning was invented."
   },
   {
    "role": "mythFact",
    "text": "Willis Carrier designed it in 1902 so printing paper would not swell."
   },
   {
    "role": "finish",
    "text": "Add louvers, a display and a filter."
   },
   {
    "role": "congrats",
    "text": "Congratulations, you have built an air conditioner."
   }
